WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.064
I'm not a philosopher,
so I'm going to dodge

00:00:05.064 --> 00:00:08.680
the many complicated ethical
questions about cryonics,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.910
about freezing someone's body
after they die

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.833
in the hope that future science will
not just unfreeze them,

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:16.229
but also cure them of
whatever ended their life.

00:00:16.229 --> 00:00:20.030
The philosophical side of this is way
outside what I'm qualified to talk about!

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.840
And honestly, my opinion
would not be helpful.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.330
I will say that I think death is
a bad thing, and that humanity

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.729
should be trying to defeat it,
the same way we've defeated smallpox.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:32.125
But that is a controversial opinion.

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:34.144
The questions I do have for
the folks that run

00:00:34.144 --> 00:00:38.265
this actual cryonics facility
are practical and legal.

00:00:38.265 --> 00:00:41.922
As ever, this is not an advert.
The team here have no editorial control.

00:00:41.922 --> 00:00:45.725
I just got invited to see a place
where the dead are being stored

00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:47.996
in the hope of resurrection.

00:00:47.996 --> 00:00:50.744
- Currently, there's still very
few patients stored here.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:54.041
It's more than zero, but you can
count it on a very few hands.

00:00:54.041 --> 00:00:56.945
The exact number we only release
once we have a few more, to

00:00:56.965 --> 00:01:01.350
not have any potential privacy
problems, but it's very few so far.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.900
- The company's official website
describes the bodies in there as

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.140
"patients", but what's being stored
are, legally and medically, corpses.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.200
Not people.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.280
The people have been declared
legally dead, and their assets

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.979
have passed to their descendants.

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:18.730
The cryo process cannot start
until the person is declared dead,

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.800
because otherwise it's murder.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.070
So if someone who's signed up is
close to death, the company will

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:27.110
dispatch a very
non-standard ambulance.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.784
- There needs to be the least possible
delay

00:01:29.784 --> 00:01:33.750
between the heart stops and the
cool-down procedure being started.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:38.237
And of course, only if the patient has
given informed consent before dying.

00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:41.510
And then we start practically
three procedures at the same time.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.670
One is we start cooling
down as quick as possible.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.266
There's a saying in medicine
that says:

00:01:46.266 --> 00:01:48.985
"you're only dead if you're
warm and dead".

00:01:48.985 --> 00:01:54.804
The metabolic rate of the cell
goes down and the cell dies slower.

00:01:54.804 --> 00:01:59.774
We also start very aggressive, what
is similar to CPR, chest compressions,

00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:04.504
giving oxygen and so on, to give
some amount of metabolic support.

00:02:04.504 --> 00:02:07.272
And before you cross 0°C,

00:02:07.272 --> 00:02:10.297
you want to replace all the blood
and the rest of the water

00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:12.715
against a medical grade antifreeze.

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:15.980
It's very important to say:
we don't freeze anything.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.679
Though, of course, the headline
always says

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:19.770
"this organization is freezing people"
and so on!

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.296
In more technical terms, we vitrify
them,

00:02:22.296 --> 00:02:25.542
which is a glass-like amorphous state,

00:02:25.542 --> 00:02:30.970
which allows tissue to be stored
without any further damage, in a state

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:36.945
where potentially future technology
might be able to, well, resuscitate.

00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:41.345
- The cryo process causes massive, probably
unrecoverable damage to the body.

00:02:41.345 --> 00:02:44.784
There is no technology, now or
even vaguely on the horizon,

00:02:44.784 --> 00:02:46.347
that could reverse that damage.

00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:49.790
But whatever is left: patient, corpse,
assembly of frozen stuff,

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.520
gets stacked up in stainless steel containers here,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.329
submerged in liquid nitrogen, until...

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:58.660
Well, until it's possible to recover
them, which could very well be never.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.330
So, obvious question, how does a dead
person pay for a service that,

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:05.480
if all goes to plan,
will continue indefinitely?

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.694
- While someone is alive, there's a small
membership fee with us,

00:03:08.694 --> 00:03:12.000
and then at the time of death,
it's around €200,000.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.709
But this is a one time fee.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.019
The largest part is given to
a non-profit organisation.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:19.775
They invest that money,

00:03:19.775 --> 00:03:24.868
with around 1-2% above inflation
in return expectations,

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:27.280
sufficient then to
maintain cryopreservation.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.464
And you have that money that you
put aside,

00:03:29.464 --> 00:03:31.604
inflation adjusted in the future,

00:03:31.604 --> 00:03:35.391
for, well, whatever medical
procedure might be necessary,

00:03:35.391 --> 00:03:37.899
to pay for reintegration, for retraining,

00:03:37.899 --> 00:03:39.819
to get used to the future again.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.822
One of the goals of our organization
is to, over time, not only make

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:47.090
the cryopreservation procedures
better, but also make it

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.605
significantly more affordable.

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:55.085
Somewhere in the mid-five-figure range,
maybe, should be possible over time.

00:03:55.085 --> 00:03:57.725
- A reminder, that's the CEO of the company.

00:03:57.725 --> 00:03:59.755
His view is going to be biased.

00:03:59.755 --> 00:04:03.347
But one of the reasons I was comfortable
filming here is that

00:04:03.347 --> 00:04:06.604
he was very candid about
the chances of this working.

00:04:06.604 --> 00:04:11.081
- We would never state that there's
any "X percent" that this works.

00:04:11.081 --> 00:04:13.954
Cryopreservation is not
a medical procedure.

00:04:13.954 --> 00:04:15.475
It's a research procedure.

00:04:15.475 --> 00:04:19.965
No one in the world can say if and
when cryopreservation might work.

00:04:19.965 --> 00:04:24.000
Long term storage is always
done with foundations.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.805
We have bylaws and statutes that make
the foundation,

00:04:27.805 --> 00:04:29.951
from a day-to-day perspective,

00:04:29.951 --> 00:04:34.985
very slow, very sluggish, and
there's a lot of inertia to change stuff.

00:04:34.985 --> 00:04:38.095
All of that is there to
maximize for longevity.

00:04:38.095 --> 00:04:41.575
I would like to live on as long as I can,
and as long as I want to.

00:04:41.575 --> 00:04:45.304
I don't only want that for me, but I
think that should be everybody's right.

00:04:45.304 --> 00:04:48.705
Ideally, I would never
like to be cryopreserved.

00:04:48.705 --> 00:04:52.745
I would like to do whatever treatments
there will be when I'm 95, and

00:04:52.745 --> 00:04:57.195
whatever medication I need to take
when I'm 60, to then make that happen.

00:04:57.195 --> 00:05:00.724
Should I die at 60 of a heart attack
or something like that, then

00:05:00.724 --> 00:05:06.075
I'd much rather be cryopreserved
than being cremated or buried.

00:05:06.075 --> 00:05:07.853
It might turn out it never works,

00:05:07.853 --> 00:05:15.130
but the alternative has as close to zero
a chance as you can ever have.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:19.460
- This, right now, is an option for
a tiny minority of rich people,

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.260
and it probably won't work.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.567
But I do think that humanity should
be trying to defeat death,

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:28.849
so I find it difficult to argue against
someone who'd take this option...

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:30.539
when I know what the alternative is.

